  "query_preview": "..Nearly zonal flow and normal hghts have brought a \nmuch quieter, dry and nearly windless day to our area today. This \ntrend will be short-lived, as a strong trough enters the west via \ncentral California later tonight. This trough will track basically \nwestward through the Great Basin Thursday, shearing apart over the \nRockies on Friday.\n\nExpect southwest winds to increase again on Thursday in advance of \nthis trough, with gusts reaching 30 to near 40 mph in most places. \nHeadlines are not needed at this time, as only a few locations \nnortheast of the Mogollon Rim will briefly touch the lower end \nof Wind Advisory criteria.\n\nBy Thursday night, the attendant cold front will move through \nnorthern Arizona, but most dynamics, moisture and colder air aloft \nare north of Arizona. \n\nHowever, the Kaibab Plateau will be closest to these ingredients \nand will have a decent upslope component. There should be enough \nsaturation for some showers overnight Thursday night into Friday \nmorning, along with falling snow levels. For now, we are currently \nforecasting 1-3 inches of snow above 6500-7000 feet for that time \nperiod, enough for some minor impacts, but no headlines.\n\nFarther to the south, saturation becomes shallow quite quickly and \nwe expect just a trace or a few tenths from the South Rim to the \nFlagstaff area. By Friday afternoon, we are back in dry and zonal \nflow heading into the weekend and early next week.\n\nA few passing s/wvs in this flow may lower temperatures and kick up \nwinds around Monday and Wednesday of next week. However, moisture \nprofiles look dry, so just slight chances of rain and snow showers \nfor now.",
